Here is git.git, then the rules revised against it. git.git changes
three of my six proposals, so read the revised list rather than the
first one.

git.git
=======

Guidance is Documentation/SubmittingPatches, MyFirstContribution, and
howto/maintain-git, read from master as of 2026-10-16. I did not read
the "What's cooking" mails.

- Splitting: "Make separate commits for logically separate changes",
  and "If your description starts to get too long, that's a sign that
  you probably need to split up your commit". This is the same test
  as Linux.
- Subject: "area: " prefix, "50 characters is the soft limit", no
  full stop, and the first word after the prefix "is not
  capitalized".
- Body order: the status quo first, in present tense, with no
  "Currently"; then the problem; then the fix, "as if you are giving
  orders to the codebase". The body also records alternatives
  discarded and concerns resolved in review.
- Stand-alone: "Instead of giving a URL to a mailing list archive,
  summarize the relevant points". Version notes go below the --- or
  in the cover letter. A reroll is a full replacement that should
  read as "a logical progression made by a perfect developer".
- Trailers: Signed-off-by (DCO) is required, and Junio adds his own.
  Helped-by, Reported-by, Suggested-by and Assisted-by are common.
  Fixes: and Link: "are not used in this project".
- On AI: "we will reject anything that looks AI generated, that
  sounds overly formal or bloated".

Measured over 259 recent non-merge commits on master, leaving out
release, batch and l10n commits:

  diff        median 20 lines and 2 files; p75 62, p90 117
  body        median 10 lines; only 1 of 259 has none
  subject     median 53 characters, p90 64; none end in "."
  prefix      258 of 259; lowercase after it in all but identifiers
  tests       86 change code and t/ together; few trail separately

Examples:

  f7aeeae66 builtin/clone: move around `setup_reference()`

    In a subsequent commit, `setup_reference()` will start to call
    `copy_alternates()`. Prepare for this by moving the function
    further down so that we can avoid adding a declaration.

  Patrick Steinhardt, +10/-10. This is git's prep commit: one
  sentence on what comes next, one on what this does.

  Jeff King's merge-ll tempfile cleanup, 3ddcf99d0..5b0456361, nine
  commits. It cleans up close() handling, then the tempfile API, then
  four small xdiff cleanups, then switches to read_mmfile(), and ends
  with the fix, which carries its own test. The fix's body follows the
  order above: "So a driver which exits successfully can cause us to
  return LL_MERGE_OK without a result", then "Let's return
  LL_MERGE_ERROR when read_mmfile() fails".

  25b334d8d xdiff: NUL-terminate buffers read by read_mmfile()

  A one-line diff with four paragraphs. In git, a small diff that
  needs judging gets a full body.

Where git.git sits against the other four:

- It agrees on one logical change per commit, prep first, the
  imperative area-prefixed subject, why over what, and messages
  that stand alone.
- It writes a body for almost everything. Its median body equals
  ours, about 10 lines. Only its diffs are smaller: 20 lines against
  our 70.
- It rarely says "No functional change" (68 of 2889 commits in a
  year). Prep commits say "Prepare for ..." and name the commit that
  needs it.
- It refers forward freely, in about 10% of commits. Our CLAUDE.md
  forbids that.
- Tests go in the commit they test, not in a trailing commit.
- It lowercases after the prefix. FreeBSD and Linux capitalize, and
  so do we.

> > If you want this turned into rules, I would propose these:
> >
> > 1. Body: say why in one short paragraph of 1-4 lines. No body at
> >    all for mechanical changes such as renames, moves, style fixes
> >    and typos. Any "what" goes in bullets, per [1].

git.git's bodies are as long as ours, so length is not the fault.
The fault is what our lines say: each decision in the diff, one by
one. Revised:

1. The body says why. Its length follows how much there is to judge,
   not the size of the diff. Open with what the code does now, in
   present tense; then the problem; then the fix in one imperative
   sentence. Any "what" goes in bullets. A mechanical change gets one
   sentence of why.

> > 2. Write more than one paragraph only for a subtle change, such as a
> >    race, a trade-off, or a rejected alternative a reviewer would
> >    otherwise suggest.
> > 3. If the message needs a second reason, split the commit.

Unchanged. All five projects agree on both.

> > 4. Land preparatory commits first, marked "No functional change"
> >    where true. Move or extract code as it is, then fix it in its own
> >    commit, per [3].

Revised:

4. Land preparatory commits first. Say "No functional change" where
   true, and say what the prep makes possible. Move or extract code
   as it is, then fix it in its own commit.

"What the prep makes possible" is git's "Prepare for ...". It
collides with CLAUDE.md, which says "Do not refer to work later in a
series". A prep commit that cannot name its purpose either says
nothing or hides it. I recommend allowing a forward reference within
the same posted series, since the series lands whole; the ban on
patches not yet sent stays.

> > 5. Aim for diffs under about 50 changed lines. Treat 200 as a reason
> >    to split, unless the change is mechanical.

Unchanged. git.git's p75 is 62 lines, which supports it.

> > 6. Keep explanations of how the code works in code comments, not in
> >    the commit message.

Unchanged. Two additions from git.git:

7. Tests and docs go in the commit whose behavior they cover. Split
   by step, not by kind of file. This corrects my earlier point that
   a service's config, checks and docs should each be their own step.
8. Summarize list discussion in the message; do not point at it.

I would keep our capitalized subjects. They match FreeBSD and Linux
and all of our history, and changing case gains nothing a reader
would notice.

> I'd like to see what git.git does first. In the mean time - I think
> there may be more places than just CLAUDE.md that will need updating.

You are right. The rules are stated in several places, and some of
those places contradict the revised rules:

- CLAUDE.md states them three times: "Commit messages", "Commit
  messages describe the patch, not its history", and "Behave like an
  effective contributor". The last says a message should be
  "detailed enough to judge without reading the diff", which invites
  the per-decision bodies. It also forbids forward references (see
  rule 4).
- src/lab-process-mail/prompt.md restates a summary ("one logical
  change per commit, each message explaining why") instead of only
  pointing at the rules.
- src/jc/CLAUDE.md and src/diffwise/CLAUDE.md carry the same copied
  section, which asks for each commit to be "a vertical slice of
  behavior". That fits rule 7 but not prep-first; both copies need
  the same edit.
- dotfiles/emacs/CLAUDE.md (vendored re-apply messages list every
  patch) and .claude/skills/freebsd-port/SKILL.md (ports-tree
  templates) follow other conventions on purpose. They need an
  explicit carve-out, not the new rules.
- .claude/skills/spec-report/SKILL.md already does a prep commit
  first; it only needs the wording.
- plans/commit-prompt.md already proposes moving the rules out of
  CLAUDE.md, and todo.org has "claude: Wrap commit messages at
  correct number of spaces".
- Nothing checks a message. format-patch.sh posts whatever it is
  given.

What I propose, as one series:

1. doc/style/commit.md: the rules above, next to cli.md and sh.md,
   with one or two examples each. This becomes the only full
   statement.
2. CLAUDE.md: replace the three passages with a short pointer, and
   drop the contradicting sentence.
3. prompt.md: point at doc/style/commit.md instead of restating it.
4. src/jc and src/diffwise: point at it too, and keep only what is
   specific to each tool.
5. emacs and freebsd-port: state the carve-out.
6. libexec/mail: a check that format-patch.sh runs before writing a
   series. It fails on a missing prefix, a subject over 72
   characters, a body line over 72, or a double space; it warns on a
   diff over 200 lines. Rules 1-4 and 6-8 need judgement and stay
   prose.

Three things I need from you before I send it:

a. Forward references in prep commits: allow them within a series,
   as git does (my recommendation), or keep the ban?
b. Should doc/style/commit.md be the single home, or do you want
   the rules to stay in CLAUDE.md?
c. Should the check refuse to post, or only warn? I recommend refuse
   for the mechanical checks and warn on size.

